// Crime: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== crime.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Blue Boats
genre: crime + domestic-drama
tone: Sunlit, fast and fatalistic
era: 1978 | period
setting: The waterfront quarters of Naples | real | city
logline: The best speedboat pilot among Naples's cigarette smugglers, a trade so ordinary her whole street lives on it, is told that the boats will carry something else from now on.
premise: At night the blue boats run out to freighters beyond the limit and come back loaded with American cigarettes, chased by customs launches that never quite catch them. Old women sell the cartons from folding tables. It is illegal and nobody thinks it is a crime. {nunzia}, twenty-four, has the fastest hands on a throttle in the bay. The men who own the boats have started meeting Sicilians.
synopsis: {nunzia} pilots because her father did and drowned doing it; the boat is how her mother, three brothers and half a stairwell eat. The first act is the trade as a way of life: the dawn race home, the finance police captain {de_rosa} who waves as he fails to catch her, the street's table economy run by her mother {assunta} with a cashbox and a rosary. The second act is the change. {don_gennaro}, who has organised the quarter's boats for twenty years and settled its quarrels, announces a new cargo: the same run, small parcels, ten times the money. {nunzia} is not naive; she refuses, and is reminded whose boat it is. Her youngest brother {ciro}, seventeen, says yes in her place. The street does not change all at once. A table closes. A boy she knew is found in a car. {de_rosa} stops waving and tells her, standing in her mother's kitchen with his cap off, that he let cigarettes go because a city has to eat, and that he will not let this go, and that she should think about which boats he will be shooting at. She tries to get {ciro} out and learns what he now owes. In the third act she takes the run herself, in his place, one last time, to buy his debt back, and makes a different delivery: she leads the customs launch, at full speed, through the mussel beds only she knows, onto the mother ship. The parcels go into the bay. {don_gennaro} understands at once who did it and why, and does the arithmetic of what killing the street's favourite daughter would cost him. She is left alive and without a boat. The tables are still there in the morning, fewer of them. {assunta} sets hers out and puts a chair beside it.
question: Where is the line between a crime everyone lives on and one that eats them?
themes: informal economies and their rules; loyalty to a street; the moment a trade becomes something else
audience: Adults who like crime stories about ordinary people
viewpoint: With {nunzia}, at water level
stakes: Her brother, her boat, a quarter's livelihood and its soul
conflict: A smuggler with a code against bosses who have found a better cargo and a brother who wants the money
ending: She wrecks the first shipment and survives it. The boat is gone; the brother is out; the trade she loved is ending either way.
experience: The joy of speed over water, a souring you feel street by street, and a last run that is all nerve
look: Peeling pastel, laundry, blue hulls on a blue bay; night runs lit only by wake and cigarette ends
sound: Twin outboards flat out, radios, Neapolitan song from windows. A customs siren that used to be a joke.
m: protect | family | loyalty-vs-truth | pyrrhic
@ nunzia | Nunzia Esposito | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 24 | To keep the boat and keep it clean | Her father died for cigarettes, not for this | The boat is not hers and her brother has said yes | Fearless on water, cautious on land | From the trade's star to the one who sinks its future | Fast Neapolitan, laughs with her whole face | Rolled jeans, her father's watch, salt-stiff hair
@ don_gennaro | Gennaro Sorrentino | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 61 | To stay in charge of what is coming anyway | He kept the peace on cigarettes for twenty years | The Sicilians do not need him for long | Courteous, paternal, realistic | From patron to a man doing cold sums | Soft, proverbial, never raises his voice | Linen jacket, espresso he never finishes
@ ciro | Ciro Esposito | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 17 | Real money, now | He is tired of being the little brother | He does not know what he owes until he does | Cocky, sweet, out of his depth | From yes to hiding in his mother's kitchen | Bravado, then nothing | New trainers, a gold chain too heavy for him
@ de_rosa | Captain Aldo De Rosa | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 45 | To stop the one thing worth stopping | He has looked away from cigarettes on purpose | He needs a smuggler's help | Wry, patient, harder than he seems | From waving at her wake to following it | Dry Roman irony | Grey uniform, sunglasses pushed up
@ assunta | Assunta Esposito | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 50 | Her table, her children, in that order of ease | She buried a husband who did this | She knows exactly what her son has done | Sharp, devout, unshockable | From counting cartons to setting out an extra chair | Bargains with saints and customers alike | Black dress, apron pocket of change
~ nunzia - ciro | sister and brother | she has always covered for him | she takes his run and his debt
~ nunzia - don_gennaro | pilot and patron | his favourite | a debt neither can call in
~ nunzia - de_rosa | smuggler and the officer who chases her | a wave across the water | a wake he is meant to follow
~ nunzia - assunta | daughter and mother | cartons handed up at dawn | a chair set beside the table
# The Trade | EXT | Bay Of Naples, Open Water | DAWN | nunzia, de_rosa | Race home | A blue boat loaded to the gunwales, a customs launch behind. She cuts inside the breakwater; he lifts a hand | | 5 | water, vehicles=speedboat+customs launch, stunts=high-speed boat chase
# The Trade | EXT | Quarter Street, Folding Tables | DAY | nunzia, assunta, ciro | See who eats | Cartons up the stairs, tables out. {assunta} sells to a priest. {ciro} wants to know why he is still carrying boxes | | 4 | crowd=60, props=cigarette cartons+folding tables
# The Change | INT | Social Club, Back Room | NIGHT | nunzia, don_gennaro, ciro | Hear the new cargo | Same run, small parcels, ten times the money. {nunzia} says no. {don_gennaro} reminds her whose hull it is. {ciro} says yes | {ciro} takes the work | 6 | crowd=12
# The Change | INT | Esposito Kitchen | DAY | nunzia, de_rosa, assunta | Be warned | {de_rosa}, cap in hand: cigarettes he let pass. Not this. He tells her which boats he will be shooting at | | 5
# The Change | EXT | Quarter Street | NIGHT | nunzia, ciro, assunta | Count the cost | A table is gone. A car is cordoned at the corner. {ciro} tells her the figure he owes | {nunzia} decides to take his run | 5 | crowd=30, vehicles=police cars
# The Last Run | EXT | Bay Of Naples, Mussel Beds | NIGHT | nunzia, de_rosa | Lead them in | Full throttle through stakes only she can read, the launch on her wake, straight to the mother ship. Parcels go over the side | The shipment is lost | 7 | water, vehicles=speedboat+customs launch+freighter, stunts=night boat chase through stakes
# The Last Run | EXT | Quarter Street | DAWN | nunzia, don_gennaro, assunta | Be left alive | {don_gennaro} looks at her across the street for a long time, and goes in. {assunta} sets out the table and a second chair | | 4 | crowd=20, props=folding table+chairs
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from Naples's cigarette-smuggling boats of the 1970s, with invented people
